ll 141 would do two major  things first  landlords would need to give tenants  more notice before an eviction  30 days for a lease less than a year  60 days for a lease up to 3 years and 90  days for a longer lease  republicans claim the law is unfair to  landlords democrat assemblyman howard  watts sponsors the bill  he says too many nevadans are in danger  of being out on the street  as a result of the pandemic nevada is on  the cliff of a major eviction crisis  a gwen center and during the pandemic  found that over  half a million nevadans are a potential  risk of eviction due to the pandemic  the second portion of this bill requires  eviction records get  sealed if the tenant is affected by the  pandemic  the federal eviction moratorium expires  march 31st  in washington d.c nevada's congressional  delegation is continuing its war  on the proposed yucca mountain nuclear  waste repository  a bill sponsored by nevada democratic  congresswoman dina titus would require  the federal government to get consent  before going forward with the  construction of yucca mountain  the proposed nuclear waste dump site is  about 75 miles northeast of las vegas  representative titus says nevada's fight  finally has an  ally in the oval office this would start  the whole process  over a disposal of waste so that  everybody's at the table  and it's based on consent not just ram  down somebody's throat  of course the ally would be president  biden another democrat the rest of the  southern nevada delegation has also  signed on to the informed consent bill  as well  nevada senator catherine cortez masto  has snagged a key  subcommittee assignment she'll chair the  public lands forests and mining  subcommittee  in addition to mining the committee  helps oversee the bureau of land  management  and the blm as you'll know controls  about 80 for 85 percent of the land in  nevada  nevada's congressional delegation also  sponsoring a bill  to set aside 2 million acres of clark  county for conservation  that includes expanding red rock  national conservation area by 51  000 acres 1.3 million acres for the  desert national wildlife refuge  and another 330 thousand acres of  wilderness around the state  the nevada conservation league says it  also encourages affordable housing  if there's a desire to conserve more of  the desert more of the open space around  las vegas  why allow it why are we building out and  not up  seventy percent of clark county uh clark  county's land would be conserved  um that's a huge huge uh chunk of of  area in our county  um and you know there's ways to be to  uh grow again in a smart and sustainable  way  um and this bill uh addresses the  housing crisis that  clark county is experiencing um by  offering the development of affordable  housing for  low-income families and in fact you know  i think that there's  about 70 78 000 um  a shortage of 78 000  homes for low-income families in the  area and this legislation will help  facilitate  responsible growth in the in the valley  and the surrounding areas  and allocates specific land for  affordable housing developments the bill  is called the southern nevada economic  development and conservation act  if you're keeping score at home is more  relief finally in the works  next on politics now we'll look at the  coven relief bill passed by the house  and what might keep it from being passed  in the senate this is 8 news now  you're watching politics now  congress appears ready to pass president  joe biden's emergency covert relief  package but it will be a very close vote  in the senate  our washington d.c bureau correspondent  alexander limon  tells us what to expect moving forward  we're now on track to have enough  vaccine supply  for every adult in america by the end of  may  president joe biden says his  administration has made great strides in  dealing with the coronavirus pandemic  and he wants that progress to continue  this  is a national imperative that we get our  kids back into the classroom  safely and as soon as possible the  president says for schools and  businesses to fully reopen  and life to return to normal congress  needs to pass his 1.9 trillion dollar  american rescue plan  that's why the president worked with  senate democrats on tuesday to ensure  enough support for the package  that would deliver money for vaccines  schools unemployment  stimulus checks and more american people  are overwhelmingly in support  of this legislation a majority of  democrats independents  and in many polls republicans support  this bill  it seems the only group that doesn't  support this bill are senate republicans  and house republicans democratic leader  chuck schumer said the senate will move  forward with the bill as soon as  possible  but republicans criticized the  legislative package saying  it's full of items unrelated to the  pandemic  so we'll be fighting this in every way  that we can  it is my hope that at the end senate  republicans will unanimously oppose it  joining us now to talk about what made  it so hard to get all the senate  democrats on board alex  what's the latest part of president joe  biden's problem was those moderate  democrats in the senate like joe manchin  of west virginia and john tester of  montana for example  and what john tester told reporters is  that they weren't necessarily trying to  get down the amount of money being spent  the size of the package but rather the  issue was that they didn't feel that the  money that was being spent was targeted  enough  so that was part of the issue but it  seems that in the end they were able to  work out a deal  to get all 50 senate democrats on board  something that was  absolutely vital if this package was  going to pass because no republican  senators expressed any support for this  package  changes were made to the package to make  them  happy to go along with it what do they  do to kind of make it work  there were a couple of big changes early  on that 15  